//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 65 - VOLATILITY-SHOCK EXHAUSTION REVERSAL, LONG. A THIRD ORTHOGONAL SIGNAL CLASS FOR
// THIS LAB, BUILT AS THE MANDATE'S "ONE GENUINELY NEW MECHANISM" NOW THAT BOTH STANDING QUEUES ARE
// BLOCKED.
//
// THE STORED SCHEDULED PROMPT still describes a board state (Attack 37, "earned a filter stack") more
// than sixty attacks stale and instructs "continue numbering after 37." THE DOCS OVERRIDE IT, per the
// prompt's own instruction: Attack 41 already closed Attack 37 on cost, Attack 46 is the long champion
// (both halves clear, cold-reproduced, filters exhausted per HARD LESSON 49), and the short leg has
// failed on FIVE distinct signal shapes spanning both named classes (level/pivot: Attacks 60-63;
// momentum-exhaustion: Attack 64) and is now reported as a standing structural asymmetry, not to be
// chased with a sixth shape absent a materially different argument (Attack 64's own queue item 1). The
// remaining open queue items -- Attack 46's out-of-sample test (blocked, cannot run under BTCUSDT-only)
// and the funding-clock counter-build diagnostic (owed only IF that family is revisited) -- are not
// mandatory this cycle. With both standing queues blocked or optional, the mandate's own fallback
// applies: propose ONE genuinely new mechanism, distinct from every mechanism already on the board.
//
// ==================== WHAT THIS CLAIMS, IN ONE SENTENCE ====================
// After an outsized single-bar downside excursion (a move from the prior close to the current low far
// larger than recent average true range -- the signature of a liquidation cascade or stop-run rather
// than fresh directional information) that the SAME bar substantially rejects by closing back in the
// upper half of its own range, price tends to continue reverting toward the level it occupied just
// before the shock.
//
// ==================== WHY THIS IS A GENUINELY NEW, THIRD ORTHOGONAL CLASS ====================
// Every mechanism on this board's short leg fell into one of two classes Attack 63 named: LEVEL/PIVOT
// (a price tapping a prior high/low/swing point: Attacks 33, 34, 36, 37, 43, 44/45/46, 59, 60, 61, 62,
// 63) or MOMENTUM-INDICATOR (an oscillator threshold event: Attacks 48-52, 64). This build's arming
// condition is neither: it is a VOLATILITY-MAGNITUDE event -- the CURRENT bar's own excursion measured
// against its OWN trailing ATR, with no reference to any prior swing high/low, resistance/support
// level, or oscillator value. The only prior-bar reference is the ATR baseline itself (a measure of
// recent noise, not a level) and the pre-shock close (the target, which HARD LESSON 41 requires be a
// level rather than a stop multiple -- here it is the most natural level available: where the market
// was trading one bar ago, before the shock). Also new: this is the LAB'S FIRST mechanism built to fade
// a single-bar magnitude outlier rather than a multi-bar structure or a rare indicator threshold.
//
// ==================== MECHANICS, NO ARRAYS, NO USER-DEFINED FUNCTIONS, SINGLE BAR ====================
// atrPrior   = ta.atr(atrLen)[1]                      -- the bar's OWN volatility state is excluded from
//              its own baseline (no self-reference): the baseline is fixed as of the PRIOR bar's close.
// downShock  = close[1] - low                         -- the intrabar plunge from the last settled price
//              to this bar's own low. This is the "shock" magnitude.
// shockCond  = downShock > shockMult * atrPrior        -- the excursion must be a rare multiple of
//              recent normal volatility (shockMult, default 2.0), not an ordinary down-wick.
// rejection  = close > low + rejectFrac * (high - low)  -- the bar closes in the upper half of its own
//              range (rejectFrac, default 0.50) despite printing that low: intrabar sellers were
//              overwhelmed by intrabar buyers on the SAME bar, a same-bar rejection candle.
// goLong     = shockCond and rejection and rBig and targetOk (defined below)
// Entry fires at THIS bar's close (filled at the next bar's open by the engine's own execution model,
// identical to Attacks 44/45/46's tap-and-reclaim pattern) -- SINGLE BAR construction, no latch needed:
// every quantity read (high, low, close, close[1], atr[1]) belongs to bars already fully closed at
// decision time, so there is no repaint and no cross-bar state to manage.
// Stop: the shock bar's OWN low (LESSON 5 -- the actual extreme just tested, not an ATR distance or a
// percentage). Target: close[1], the level the market occupied immediately before the shock (HARD
// LESSON 41 -- a level, not a multiple of R; E14 -- an independent quantity from the stop distance,
// drawn from the market's own pre-shock price rather than derived from R or from the shock magnitude).
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg (LONG ONLY):
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) - EXCLUSION via rBig on rLong = close - low, never clamped. A shock bar whose
//        stop distance is too close is SKIPPED, not widened.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) - slPx = low, the actual extreme the shock bar printed.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) - LONG ONLY, fading DOWNSIDE shocks. A mirrored short (fading
//        upside shocks) is a distinct, untested candidate, not built this cycle (credit budget below).
//   BINDING (E17) - shockCond AND rejection AND rBig AND targetOk all necessarily bind; trade count is
//        read before anything else is interpreted (HARD LESSON 12), and the two substantive terms
//        (shockCond, rejection) are expected to bind hard given a 2x-ATR single-bar threshold.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) - shockCond constrains the MAGNITUDE of the down-excursion relative to recent
//        volatility (a size condition, comparing today's move to the ATR baseline); rejection
//        constrains the SHAPE of the close within the bar's own range (a position condition,
//        independent of size -- a huge-range bar can close anywhere in its range, and a modest-range
//        bar can close at its high). rBig separately constrains the ABSOLUTE stop distance in %.
//        targetOk only confirms positive reward remains; it adds no additional selection beyond that.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) - not applicable: this is a SINGLE-BAR construction like Attacks
//        44/45/46, not a multi-bar arm/trigger pattern. All referenced quantities (high, low, close of
//        the signal bar; close[1] and atr[1] from the prior bar) are already fixed and non-repainting
//        the instant the signal bar closes; entry fires at that close and fills next bar, so setup and
//        fill cannot share a bar by the engine's own execution model.
//   OCCUPANCY (LESSONS 24/28/29) - pyramiding=1, `flat` gates every entry; maxBars=192 caps the hold
//        exactly as every other build in this lab.
//   CASCADE / MARGIN ARTIFACT (HARD LESSON 42/43) - LONG at 100% equity, the same sizing every accepted
//        long build in this lab uses (Attacks 44/45/46); the declared-deviation fix is a SHORT-side
//        margin-formula artifact only (HARD LESSON 42) and does not apply here. VERIFY with get_trades
//        before trusting the result: cascadeRatio should read 1 / maxCascadeDepth 1, as every LONG
//        build in this lab has shown.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale.
//
// CODE BARE - NO ADDED FILTER BEYOND THE MECHANISM ITSELF (no RR floor, no regime gate, no time-of-day
// filter): the mandate requires a bare mechanism on a fresh design, with any filter stack to follow
// only if both halves clear 1.0 on a defensible sample, exactly as Attack 37 earned its (later
// exhausted, then closed) filter-stack queue.
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17):
//   * H1 (2022-01-01 to 2024-06-08, never-tuned) BELOW 1.0 -> DISCARDED by the kill rule. No filters,
//     no rescue, no H2 spent -- one more falsified mechanism, recorded plainly.
//   * H1 ABOVE 1.0 AND H2 ABOVE 1.0, BOTH IN THE ~60-350 WORKABLE BAND -> this lab's second candidate
//     ever to clear both halves (after Attack 37, since closed, and Attack 46, the long champion),
//     queued for RATCHET v2 filter work.
//   * TRADES WELL ABOVE THE WORKABLE BAND -> reframe as a frequency/cost miss (HARD LESSON 4), not an
//     edge verdict, as Attacks 59 and 63 were.
//   * TRADES WELL BELOW ~30 -> a DIRECTION, not a result (LESSON 12); a 2x-ATR single-bar shock with a
//     50%-range rejection may simply be too rare on 15m BTC to sample cleanly, in which case shockMult
//     is the first parameter to relax, not the mechanism to abandon.
//   * A MAJORITY WIN RATE WITH A POOR PAYOFF -> the inverted-payoff shape, HARD LESSON 53's now-numbered
//     pattern (Attacks 50/51/59/63), recurring a fifth time; report explicitly rather than as novel.
//   * A MINORITY WIN RATE WITH A NEAR-EVEN PAYOFF -> the plain negative-edge shape (Attack 64); a
//     distinct failure mode from the above, counted separately per HARD LESSON 53's own closing note.
//   * LOSS DISTANCES SCATTERED AND SUB-0.8% -> would indicate a harness artifact rather than a
//     measurement (HARD LESSON 42/43's tell), though not expected on a 100%-equity long.
//   REPORT GROSS, COMMISSION, ACHIEVED RATIO, AVG LOSER AND WIN RATE alongside net (LESSONS 36/39 and
//        the board's own three-drawdown-category taxonomy).

strategy("BTC Attack65 shock exhaustion reversal LONG", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

atrLen    = input.int(14,   "ATR length for the shock baseline")
shockMult = input.float(2.0,"Shock threshold -- multiple of prior ATR the down-excursion must exceed")
rejectFrac= input.float(0.50,"Rejection fraction -- close must sit above low + this fraction of the bar's range")
minRpct   = input.float(0.80,"Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars   = input.int(192,  "Max bars in trade")

atrPrior  = ta.atr(atrLen)[1]
downShock = close[1] - low
shockCond = not na(atrPrior) and downShock > shockMult * atrPrior

barRange  = high - low
rejection = barRange > 0 and close > low + rejectFrac * barRange

rLong   = close - low
minR    = close * minRpct / 100
rBig    = rLong >= minR

targetLevel = close[1]
targetOk    = targetLevel > close

goLong = shockCond and rejection and rBig and targetOk

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

if flat and goLong
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := low
    tpPx   := targetLevel
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(targetLevel, title="TargetLevel(PreShockClose)")
plot(atrPrior, title="ATRPrior")
